body:
## Summary

`ColumnView.flattenLists()` (which routes to `cudf::lists::concatenate_list_elements`) triggers `cudaErrorIllegalAddress` (CUDA error 700) when the input is a `list<list<T>>` column whose inner `list<T>` child has **0 rows** — i.e., the outer list row is a valid-but-empty list (`[]`).

The CUDA error is asynchronous and deferred: `flattenLists()` returns without throwing, but the next GPU operation that forces a stream sync (allocation, copy, etc.) surfaces the fatal error.

## Environment

- cuDF version: 26.06.00
- spark-rapids-jni: 26.06.0-SNAPSHOT (bundles cuDF)
- GPU: NVIDIA RTX PRO 4500 Blackwell Server Edition
- CUDA driver: 590.44.01
- Java: OpenJDK 17.0.18

## Reproducer

```java
import ai.rapids.cudf.*;
import java.util.*;

public class FlattenReproducer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        HostColumnVector.ListType intType =
            new HostColumnVector.ListType(true, new HostColumnVector.BasicType(true, DType.INT32));
        HostColumnVector.ListType listOfListsType =
            new HostColumnVector.ListType(true, intType);

        // PASS: normal non-empty case
        try (ColumnVector input = ColumnVector.fromLists(listOfListsType,
                 Arrays.asList(Arrays.asList(1, 2), Arrays.asList(3)));
             ColumnVector result = input.flattenLists()) {
            Cuda.deviceSynchronize(); // OK
            System.out.println("flatten([[1,2],[3]]): OK, rows=" + result.getRowCount());
        }

        // PASS: null inner list
        try (ColumnVector input = ColumnVector.fromLists(listOfListsType,
                 Arrays.asList((List<Integer>) null));
             ColumnVector result = input.flattenLists()) {
            Cuda.deviceSynchronize(); // OK
            System.out.println("flatten([null]): OK, rows=" + result.getRowCount());
        }

        // CRASH: outer list is a valid-but-empty list — inner column has 0 rows
        try (ColumnVector input = ColumnVector.fromLists(listOfListsType,
                 Collections.emptyList());        // 1 row: []
             ColumnVector result = input.flattenLists()) {
            Cuda.deviceSynchronize(); // <-- cudaErrorIllegalAddress 700 here
            System.out.println("flatten([]): OK");
        }
    }
}
```

## Actual output

```
flatten([[1,2],[3]]): OK, rows=1
flatten([null]): OK, rows=1
Testing flatten([]) ... [fromLists OK] [flattenLists returned] FAILED at deviceSynchronize after flattenLists:
Fatal CUDA error encountered at: ../../thirdparty/cudf/java/src/main/native/src/CudaJni.cpp:456:
700 cudaErrorIllegalAddress an illegal memory access was encountered
```

## Expected output

```
flatten([]): OK, rows=1   (result should be a 1-row column containing an empty list)
```

## Root cause analysis

The only guard in `cudf::lists::concatenate_list_elements` (`concatenate_list_elements.cu`) is:

```cpp
if (input.size() == 0) { return cudf::empty_like(input); }
```

This guards against a **zero-row outer column** but not against the case where the outer column has rows yet the **inner `list<T>` child column has 0 rows** (all outer rows are empty lists).

When the inner column has 0 rows, `concatenate_lists_ignore_null` proceeds to access device memory via `d_list_offsets` (derived from the inner column's offsets child). For a 0-row list column, this offsets buffer may be uninitialized or zero-length, causing the illegal memory access in the kernel.

The existing Java test `ColumnVectorTest::testFlattenLists` does not cover any empty-inner-list case.

## Workaround

In `spark-rapids`, `GpuFlattenArray.doColumnar` now guards against this before calling `flattenLists()`:

```scala
val needsEmptyGuard = withResource(inputBase.getChildColumnView(0)) { inner =>
  inner.getRowCount == 0L || { ... }
}
if (!needsEmptyGuard) inputBase.flattenLists
else /* build empty-list column directly */
```

See: https://github.com/NVIDIA/spark-rapids/pull/14610

## Suggested fix

Add a guard in `cudf::lists::concatenate_list_elements` (or `concatenate_lists_ignore_null`) for the case `lists_column_view(input).child().size() == 0`, returning `cudf::empty_like(input)` (with outer null mask preserved). Also extend `ColumnVectorTest::testFlattenLists` to cover `[]`, `[[]]`, and `[[],[]]` inputs.
